yield: for 400g pork belly
ingredients:
  - item: pork belly
    mass: 400
    basis: true
    note: or spare rib strips, cubed
  - item: veg oil
    note: amount tbd during caramelising
  - item: sugar
    mass: 20
  - item: onion
    note: chopped
  - item: dark soy
    qty: 0.2
    unit: cup
  - item: light soy
    qty: 0.2
    unit: cup
  - item: shaoxing
    qty: 0.25
    unit: cup
  - item: water
    note: top up as needed
steps:
  - blanch pork belly cubes for a few mins and drain
  - add veg oil and sugar to a 160°C pan to melt and start caramelising (few mins)
  - add chopped onion and cook for a few mins until translucent
  - add pork
  - add dark soy, light soy, and shaoxing
  - top up with water
  - cover with lid and simmer for 1.5 hours, stirring every 15 mins to prevent burning
  - remove lid and cook down to thicken sauce
components:
  - name: variant (fallow — dry caramel, aromats not onion)
    ingredients:
      - item: pork belly
        note: diced into ~2cm chunks
      - item: palm sugar
        note: more common in Chinese cooking; light brown or caster sugar both work
      - item: shaoxing
        qty: 30
        unit: ml
      - item: light soy
        qty: 2
        unit: part
      - item: dark soy
        qty: 1
        unit: part
        note: for the rich colour and the heavy saltiness
      - item: ginger
        note: sliced
      - item: star anise
        qty: 1
        unit: ea
      - item: spring onion
        note: cut into ~4cm chunks, into the braise
      - item: green onion
        note: fresh, stirred through at the end
      - item: water
        note: only 3/4 up the belly, not covering
    steps:
      - blanch the diced belly in boiling water ~3 mins to render a little fat and start the cook
      - drain, pat dry on paper, tip the water out and put the same pan back on the heat
      - palm sugar into the dry pan on low heat, melt until it turns a dark rich caramel
      - pork back in, cook out 1-2 mins to boil off the water still clinging to it
      - shaoxing in, then turn the heat right down
      - light and dark soy, 2:1
      - spring onion, ginger and star anise
      - water last, only 3/4 up the belly — too much and it costs you flavour
      - lid on, low heat, ~1 hr then check
      - at ~40 mins the soy has reduced but the belly still has no give under the spoon — add a touch more water and carry on
      - from ~1.5 hrs keep moving and stirring so the fat and juices homogenise into a glaze
      - fresh green onion through at the end for the raw allium hit
      - serve with steamed rice and Chinese spinach
    notes: |
      Fallow's level-1 belly. Same dish as the version above, but three changes worth stealing
      even if you cook the original: a dry caramel taken properly dark before the pork goes in,
      aromats (ginger, star anise, spring onion) in place of the brown onion, and much less
      liquid.

      The dry caramel is the real one. Melting sugar alone lets you see the colour and take it
      to a dark bitter-edged caramel; sugar melted into oil with onion softening in it never
      gets there, and you end up with sweetness rather than the faintly burnt-toffee bitterness
      that stops red-braise being cloying. Trade-off is that dry caramel goes from dark to acrid
      in seconds — have the pork within reach.

      Damp pork into hot caramel will spit. That's what the 1-2 min cook-out is for; the water
      has to go before anything else does.

      Water only 3/4 up, not covering. Less liquid to drive off at the end means the sauce
      arrives at a glaze while the belly is arriving at tender, instead of you boiling hard at
      the finish to catch up.

      2:1 light:dark soy is the ratio to hold onto. Dark soy is for colour and a heavier salt,
      not for the primary seasoning — leading with it gives you something that looks right and
      tastes flat and slightly bitter.
notes: |
  Jap variant:
  - Replace shaoxing with sake
  - Add 1/8 cup mirin
  - Drop soys to 1/8 cup each
  - Add a teaspoon/tbsp of miso
